WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.280
ED GLAESER: Tell us about Airbnb, how do you get involved with that company?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.835
MICHAEL LUCA: So eBay and Amazon started in the mid

00:00:05.835 --> 00:00:08.998
1990s, bunches of others started selling products around the same time.

00:00:08.998 --> 00:00:14.186
In fact Match.com started thinking about how to facilitate dates in the mid 1990s.

00:00:14.186 --> 00:00:16.971
So there are implications of platforms like eBay and

00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:19.881
Amazon, one is that markets would be more efficient so

00:00:19.881 --> 00:00:23.571
prices might come down, your availability to products might go up.

00:00:23.571 --> 00:00:27.329
But in addition to the efficiency implications, people had explored

00:00:27.329 --> 00:00:30.587
the equity implications of platforms like Ebay and Amazon.

00:00:30.587 --> 00:00:35.013
What they had thought about is not only will markets become more efficient, but

00:00:35.013 --> 00:00:37.935
people posited that markets would also become fairer.

00:00:37.935 --> 00:00:41.355
There was a great paper that looked at transactions for

00:00:41.355 --> 00:00:43.232
cars that initiated online.

00:00:43.232 --> 00:00:47.040
And they had hypothesized that by buying this online where you're not kind

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:47.970
of face to face, and

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.090
looking at the person, that you might get rid of a lot of the discrimination.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.930
And in fact, what they found is that in offline purchases,

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.535
they saw the same types of discrimination that they had seen before.

00:00:57.535 --> 00:01:03.571
But in online purchases they had seen most of this discrimination had gone away.

00:01:03.571 --> 00:01:05.607
And when you look at a platform like Airbnb,

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:07.929
they're making very different design choices.

00:01:07.929 --> 00:01:10.608
They're showing you not only the listing where you might stay, but

00:01:10.608 --> 00:01:12.305
the person you might stay with.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:17.908
They're giving hosts a lot of flexibility about who was going to stay with them.

00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:23.479
And we ran an experiment in which we tested for discrimination

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.299
on the platform, and we found that, GLAESER: How did you

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:27.848
do that? LUCA: So

00:01:27.848 --> 00:01:31.696
we have followed an autostudy methodology that really within economics have been

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.460
pioneered by Marianne Bertrand and Sendhil Mullainathan.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.840
Where we have looked at the names of the guests that were

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.114
going to be staying somewhere.

00:01:42.114 --> 00:01:46.187
So we took names that were statistically most likely to be a white person, and

00:01:46.187 --> 00:01:49.920
statistically most likely to be African-American person.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.390
And what we could see is that if you have a name that is statistically more likely

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:58.810
to be African-American, that you're about 16% less likely to be accepted for

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:03.830
an Airbnb, relative to the same application with a white sounding name.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:07.240
So eventually Airbnb put together a committee.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.180
I'd spoken several times with people at Airbnb about

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.200
steps they could take to reduce discrimination.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.727
In their committee they laid out a series of changes that

00:02:15.727 --> 00:02:18.628
they were going to make to a platform to try to reduce the amount of

00:02:18.628 --> 00:02:21.225
discrimination. GLAESER: What else is going on in

00:02:21.225 --> 00:02:22.093
Airbnb? LUCA: So

00:02:22.093 --> 00:02:25.010
Airbnb has a few issues that they struggle with.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.829
The way that I think about them is, discrimination is one set of issues

00:02:28.829 --> 00:02:33.573
they've been thinking about, taxes are an issue that they've been thinking about.

00:02:33.573 --> 00:02:37.378
So thinking about someone who rents out a place as an Airbnb,

00:02:37.378 --> 00:02:40.823
should they be paying taxes as income as a individual?

00:02:40.823 --> 00:02:44.291
Should they be paying taxes at the hotel tax rate?

00:02:44.291 --> 00:02:49.176
So these are open policy questions that policy makers are thinking about and

00:02:49.176 --> 00:02:53.309
that airbnb has high powered incentives to try to shape as well.

00:02:53.309 --> 00:02:57.263
Externalities is an enormous issue that Airbnb struggles with.

00:02:57.263 --> 00:03:01.457
Thinking about an apartment building, if the person next to you takes their place

00:03:01.457 --> 00:03:04.920
and sublets it as an Airbnb, and you have a different person staying

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.423
there every night, this imposes an externality on the neighbors.

00:03:08.423 --> 00:03:10.845
So policymakers are trying to think through,

00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:14.370
how do you deal with this externality? GLAESER: Well, there are many cases, for

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.205
example, if you have a cooperative, or even in a condominium,

00:03:17.205 --> 00:03:18.688
the building can make the rules.

00:03:18.688 --> 00:03:22.552
So I certainly feel with those who don't want their neighbors renting their room as

00:03:22.552 --> 00:03:23.222
a hotel room.

00:03:23.222 --> 00:03:27.148
But presumably the coop association could just ban it without the need of actual

00:03:27.148 --> 00:03:29.098
public regulation, right? LUCA: Sure.

00:03:29.098 --> 00:03:34.177
So, buildings are making regulations in a lot of these situations.

00:03:34.177 --> 00:03:37.604
Cities are also getting involved in what the regulation should be.

00:03:37.604 --> 00:03:42.395
And one feature of the digital age is lots of people who moved into a place weren't

00:03:42.395 --> 00:03:46.977
necessarily thinking about even the potential of a platform like Airbnb when

00:03:46.977 --> 00:03:49.109
they purchased a place, so right?

00:03:49.109 --> 00:03:52.234
So if short term rentals were allowed in 1995,

00:03:52.234 --> 00:03:55.215
that may have felt very different than short term

00:03:55.215 --> 00:03:57.735
rentals being allowed in 2015. GLAESER: Do you

00:03:57.735 --> 00:04:01.351
think cities are differentially effected by these new tech-networks relative to

00:04:01.351 --> 00:04:02.366
other places? LUCA: So,

00:04:02.366 --> 00:04:06.941
when we think about some of the first term generations of online platforms,

00:04:06.941 --> 00:04:09.168
think about Skype, Amazon, eBay.

00:04:09.168 --> 00:04:11.981
These disproportionately you might have thought help people living

00:04:11.981 --> 00:04:12.812
outside of cities.

00:04:12.812 --> 00:04:16.267
Somebody who can now order something from a city that otherwise would have been

00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:16.957
unavailable.

00:04:16.957 --> 00:04:20.593
Somebody who could telecommute from a place that previously would have been

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:21.399
inaccessible.

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:25.338
But in recent years what we've actually seen is a series of platforms that have

00:04:25.338 --> 00:04:27.405
popped up that require network effect.

00:04:27.405 --> 00:04:31.940
So they need a certain amount of market thickness for them to successfully launch

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.058
in an area, and those platforms-- GLAESER: Why Uber starts in San Francisco,

00:04:35.058 --> 00:04:36.011
right? LUCA: Exactly,

00:04:36.011 --> 00:04:38.749
why Uber starts in San Francisco, and why so

00:04:38.749 --> 00:04:43.782
many platforms start in San Francisco and New York, and not in more rural areas.

00:04:43.782 --> 00:04:47.798
In that world there's been a benefit that at least thus far has

00:04:47.798 --> 00:04:50.820
disproportionately accrued to urban areas.

